uh  and the reputation of canada throughout  the world  have been threatened consistently  in a coordinated manner  has been  receiving funding from abroad this  situation cannot be tolerated in our  country  cabinet invoked the emergencies act to  restore public order  information is now being shared  by law enforcement with canada's  financial institutions  financial service providers have already  taken action  based on that information  the emergency measures we put in place  are being used  they are having an impact  and they will have a growing impact in  the days to come  the measures we enacted  this  as well to communicate  information  and financing to unlife unlawful  blockades since monday's announcement  i've spoken directly with the heads of  our major banks  and with the director of fintrac  my cabinet colleagues and i are meeting  regularly  very regularly including with the  commissioner of the rcmp  to discuss next steps  our absolute priority is ending these  illegal blockades and occupation  it gives me no pleasure to impose any of  these measures  in fact we do so with great sorrow  but do not doubt our determination to  act  to defend our democracy  to defend our economy  and to restore peace  order  and good government  so let me repeat what i said on monday  if your truck  is being used in these protests  your corporate accounts  will be frozen  the insurance on your vehicle  will be suspended  the consequences are real  and  they will bite  it is time for you to go home  and let me also be clear that we will  have zero tolerance for the  establishment of new blockades or  occupations  we now have the tools  to follow the money  we can see what is happening  and what is being planned in real time  and we are absolutely determined that  this must end  now and for good  before turning to my colleagues i want  to make one additional but unrelated  announcement  one that will be welcome news for cities  across the country  the federal government will be providing  a one-time 750 million dollar top-up of  transit funding for municipalities to  help make up for some of the pandemic  related operating shortfalls that  transit systems have been experiencing  this funding is contingent on two  very reasonable requirements  first  that our provincial and territorial  partners  match the funding we the federal  government will be providing  and second that provinces work to speed  up their actions to improve housing  supply in collaboration with  municipalities  housing is a shared responsibility and  we all have an important role to play  federal the federal government will  provide two municipalities  750 million  funding transit systems  living conditions in our communities we  must ensure that our  municipalities are able  to face the economic repercussions that  are ongoing from the penalties  thank you very much  good thing they brought this step for  you bill  good morning beaumate  thank you for joining us today  i want to begin by highlighting two  important developments that happened  late yesterday  last night in the house of commons i  officially tabled a motion for  confirmation of the emergency  declaration under the emergencies act  and just a few minutes ago  i spoke to this motion during the debate  over the next few days i look forward to  a very vigorous exchange with members of  all parties at this important and unique  moment  along with the prime minister and my  minister colleagues we will be  explaining to canadians how we got to  this point why the emergencies act is  necessary and where we go from here  as always we will explain clearly and  openly how we're acting in the best  interests of canadians  as well yesterday afternoon i can  confirm that the emerson border crossing  officially reopened  emerson is manitoba's most important  link to the united states  a vital crossing point for travelers and  trade  and with this significant step  i'm pleased to share that all ports of  entry on the canada-us border are now  clear  of illegal blockades and open to traffic  but i also want to add that this process  is not set in progress is not set in  stone and nor should we assume that it  is linear  yesterday we saw a group of illegal  blockades attempt to return to the  ambassador bridge  to once again  choke this vital trade quarter off and  the impact that that would have had not  only in southwestern ontario but right  across the country cannot be overstated  and it can't be allowed to happen  and that's exactly why we invoked the  emergencies act  law enforcement must have all of the  tools that they need to ensure that  these crossings stay open  but i want to be clear that the very  targeted tools that we've introduced  under the emergencies act are time  limited and they are protected by the  safeguards which are enshrined in our  charter  this means that whether you are a law  enforcement person or any other  individual that is  assisting  under the emergencies act that you are  bound  to act in compliance with the charter  every day  some 50 million dollars in trade passes  through the couts crossing and at the  ambassador bridge it's nearly 400  million  it's no exaggeration to say that these  illegal blockades have already cost our  economy in the billions  but these aren't just numbers they're  jobs for canadians  it's about the rancher near lethbridge  who sells alberta beef to hungry  families in the united states or the  auto worker at the chrysler plant in  windsor who builds cars for markets  across north america  we owe it to them and to all canadians  to ensure that these vital corridors  remain open for business  there's no ordinary law  but we know that we are in no ordinary  time  as former minister perrin beatty said  when introducing it in 1988 the  emergencies act is quote  an ability to enable the federal  government to fulfill its constitutional  responsibility  to provide for safety and security of  canadians during national 